\begin_layout Section*
Résonance intérieure — l'hystérésis comme sankhara
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
La bifurcation de l'anche est le plus souvent sous-critique : il faut une
 pression plus forte pour la faire démarrer (p_on) que pour l'éteindre (p_off).
 Entre les deux, deux états coexistent — l'anche muette et l'anche qui chante
 — et le système « reste » dans celui où il se trouve.
 C'est, littéralement, la structure d'un conditionnement : le sankhara.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Une habitude, une réaction, un pli du caractère obéissent à la même loi :
 il en coûte davantage pour amorcer un état intérieur que pour le maintenir,
 et l'on y demeure accroché bien après que la cause a disparu.
 Sortir de cet état — franchir la barrière entre les deux vallées du potentiel
 — demande de l'énergie, ou du bruit : parfois c'est l'agitation de la vie,
 parfois la patience de l'observation qui finit par faire basculer.
 La largeur de l'hystérésis, p_on moins p_off, se mesure au banc ; elle
 dit quelque chose de la ténacité d'un pli.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic

\begin_inset Formula $\triangleright$
\end_inset
 ton expérience : une hystérésis intime — un état dont il fut plus dur
 d'entrer que d'y rester, ou plus dur d'en sortir que d'y demeurer.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Chapter
Temporal and frequency of free reed
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A note is not a fixed object. It is born, it drifts with the breath of the bellows, and it dies. This chapter follows the note in time and in pitch: how much its frequency moves, how long it takes to speak, how loud it can be, and how its past shapes its present. It is also where this part answers Q6 as far as it can.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Frequency shift
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every tuner knows that a reed does not keep exactly the pitch it had on the bench. Tuned when plucked or blown softly, it moves once mounted in the instrument and played at other nuances. Three shifts add up.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first shift happens without any air flowing: the tongue alone, then the same tongue on its chamber. Below the resonance of the chamber, the air that the tongue pushes into the chamber and through the hole moves with it, like a small added mass, and the pitch goes slightly down. For three reeds of the R12 reed block, the prediction is 1.2 cents lower for the bass reed, 2.8 cents for the medium one and 3.5 cents for the high one (Section Experimental model updating, gesture b).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second shift is between the plucked tongue and the played reed. Here the models do not agree, not even on the sign. The corrected single-reed model plays a little above the blade, and the shift fades towards the treble: 
\begin_inset Formula $+30$
\end_inset
 cents at 102 Hz, 
\begin_inset Formula $+2$
\end_inset
 cents at 440 Hz, nothing at 880 Hz (Table 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "tab:hauteur-languette"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset
). The reason is simple: the stiffness added by the air is roughly fixed, while the stiffness of the tongue grows like the square of its frequency. The two-reed model with a closing opening plays a few cents below the blade (
\begin_inset Formula $-4$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $-24$
\end_inset
 cents from A2 to A5), and so does the model of the slot with its supports (
\begin_inset Formula $-9$
\end_inset
 cents at 300 Pa).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The third shift is with pressure, and here all the models agree: the note goes down when the player pushes. In the corrected single-reed model the pitch drops by 15.7 cents between 1.3 and 2 times the starting flow, and by 36 to 39 cents at 4 to 16 times (where the travel of the tongue is already absurd). The model of the slot with its supports gives 
\begin_inset Formula $-9$
\end_inset
, 
\begin_inset Formula $-14$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-26$
\end_inset
 cents at 300, 1000 and 3000 Pa. The direction is the one players know; the size is to be measured.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The largest shift of all is the most mysterious. In Ewen's sandwich, the same tongue gives an A when blown and a B when sucked: a whole tone, 12 per cent in frequency, set only by the height of the supports on each side (Section The reed socket). No model of the repository explains it yet.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The measurement that decides is easy: pluck the tongue and read its pitch, then play it at several pressures read on a water manometer, and read the pitch each time with the tuner of the repository, whose precision is better than 0.1 cent (experiment E1 of the two-reed audit).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Back to Q6. For a poly-dynamic instrument, the drop of pitch with pressure is the first obstacle. A reed is tuned at one pressure; if the models are right, it is tens of cents flat at the loudest nuances. Measuring this curve, note by note, is the first step; designing a reed or a socket that flattens it would be the second.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): experiment E1 on a few reeds across the keyboard: plucked pitch, then played pitch at three or four pressures, pushing and pulling.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Response time
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The time a reed takes to speak is part of its character. Between the moment the air arrives and the moment the note is fully there, the tongue has to grow from almost nothing to its full swing. The repository measures this response time, written 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{\mathrm{resp}}$
\end_inset
, from a recording: the tool analysis.py takes it from the onset of the sound to the moment the amplitude reaches 90 per cent of its steady value.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physics behind it is a growth. Just after the threshold has been crossed, the amplitude grows like 
\begin_inset Formula $A(t)=A_{0}\,e^{\sigma t}$
\end_inset
, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset
 is the growth rate given by the stability analysis (Section A physical model for reed design) and 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{0}$
\end_inset
 the small amplitude it starts from. Going from 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{0}$
\end_inset
 to 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset
 takes the time 
\begin_inset Formula $t=\ln(A/A_{0})/\sigma$
\end_inset
. In the two-reed model with a closing opening, an A4 at 1000 Pa grows at 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma=19.0$
\end_inset
 s
\begin_inset Formula $^{-1}$
\end_inset
: about 53 ms for each factor 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset
, and about 0.36 s to grow by a factor of one thousand, 60 dB. Two consequences follow. A larger first kick, a sharper attack of the bellows or a stronger first puff, shortens the response, because 
\begin_inset Formula $A_{0}$
\end_inset
 is larger. And near the threshold, where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma$
\end_inset
 tends to zero, the response time grows without limit: the reed hesitates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This hesitation has a name: critical slowing down. Near a bifurcation, a system takes longer and longer to answer a small disturbance. It is one of the precursors that stochastic.py looks for in recordings. The model met it too: near the threshold, the oscillation took about 1.5 s to settle, and a simulation of only 0.9 s once led to the wrong conclusion that the amplitude kept growing for ever. A system that is slow to settle must be watched long enough.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): the response time of a few notes at several pressures (a microphone and analysis.py). And: were response times computed during the measurement campaigns of 2019 to 2021 (folders described in research/docs/DATA.md)? If so, where are the results?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Intensity 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Push harder and the note grows louder, until it chokes. How much louder, and at what cost in air?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The sound power comes from the oscillating flow. For a small opening in a flat baffle, the radiated power is 
\begin_inset Formula $P=\rho\,\omega^{2}Q^{2}/(4\pi c)$
\end_inset
 (Section Dissipation). This simple law holds while the opening is small compared with the wavelength: the product 
\begin_inset Formula $ka$
\end_inset
 of the wave number and the size of the opening is about 0.08 at 440 Hz, and it reaches about 0.9 near 5 kHz, where the law starts to fail.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The efficiency of a reed is the ratio of the sound power to the pneumatic power that the bellows spends, the product 
\begin_inset Formula $p\,q$
\end_inset
 of the pressure and the flow. The bench gives this product directly (impedance.py computes the impedance 
\begin_inset Formula $p/q$
\end_inset
 and the power 
\begin_inset Formula $p\,q$
\end_inset
 from the pressure and flow sensors). The models do not predict it yet. The successive versions of the model give efficiencies from 
\begin_inset Formula $1.5\cdot10^{-5}$
\end_inset
 (one reed, network with a tuned resistance) to 
\begin_inset Formula $2.9\cdot10^{-5}$
\end_inset
 (two reeds in phase) and up to 1 to 3 per cent (the closing-opening version, which is suspect because its tongues travel too far): three orders of magnitude apart. The air consumption is just as uncertain: two reeds take 1.56 times the air of one in one version, almost twice in the other. Only the flow meter can decide.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Near the threshold, one law is expected in all cases. If the bifurcation is supercritical, or weakly subcritical above its threshold, the amplitude grows like 
\begin_inset Formula $\sqrt{p-p_{\mathrm{on}}}$
\end_inset
 (experiment E5). Measured on the sound level and on the travel of the tip, it tells how fast a reed gains its voice.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Back to Q6. The dynamic range of a reed is the distance, in decibels, between its softest sound just above 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
 and its loudest sound just before choking. Together with the drop of pitch (Section Frequency shift), it is the first figure of merit of a poly-dynamic reed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): for one reed at several pressures: the sound level (the same phone, at a fixed distance, is enough for relative levels) and the flow (flow meter), to give its dynamic range, its efficiency and its consumption.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Hysteresis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The hysteresis of the reed also has a side in time. Inside the band where the reed can be silent or sounding, which state it is in depends on its history, and on chance.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
History first. The measured starting pressure depends on how fast the pressure rises. Near the threshold, the oscillation grows slowly (Section Response time), so a fast ramp passes the threshold before the oscillation has become visible, and the reed seems to start later than it really does. This is why seuil.py works on slow ramps, why ramp.py repeats them, and why every measured threshold must be given with the speed of its ramp.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Chance second. The flow is never perfectly steady: turbulence and the hand of the player add noise. Inside the bistable band, noise can flip the reed from silent to sounding and back. Each state is a valley of the effective potential, and the noise is a random shaking. The time spent in a valley before escaping grows very fast with the height of the barrier between the valleys, compared with the strength of the noise: this is the escape rate of Kramers. The tool stochastic.py rebuilds the potential from a recording, measures the residence times in each state, and computes this escape rate. A note held near 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset
 is fragile: a small change of the breath can let it drop out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\shape italic
To be completed (Ewen): 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{on}}$
\end_inset
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{\mathrm{off}}$
\end_inset
 of one reed at three ramp speeds; and one long recording with the pressure held inside the band, to count the spontaneous starts and stops.
\end_layout

